{
  "rules": [
    "Correct for severe underestimation of upward momentum: When history shows a sharp price spike (e.g., >3 EUR in 2 days) and sentiment turns positive (>=0.20), the model's flat or slightly declining forecast must be adjusted upward by 15-20% to capture the continuation trend.",
    "Apply aggressive mean reversion after peaks: The model fails to anticipate the magnitude of price drops following a rally; if the forecast predicts stability or a slow rise while the true outcome drops >10 EUR, apply a downward correction of 5-8 EUR for the subsequent 10-day horizon.",
    "Ignore sentiment lag in volatile regimes: Positive sentiment scores (0.20-0.33) appearing in the history often precede a 2-3 week rally that the model misses entirely; treat any sentiment >0.20 as a leading indicator for a 10-15 EUR price increase, regardless of the model's current flat trajectory.",
    "Adjust for horizon-specific overshoot: The model consistently underestimates the peak magnitude (overshoot) during bull runs and underestimates the depth of the subsequent crash; apply a non-linear correction that increases forecast values in the first 10 days of a rally and decreases them sharply in days 15-25.",
    "Detect structural regime shift: The model assumes a mean-reverting range (72-73 EUR) even when the market has structurally shifted to a higher floor (75+ EUR); if the last 5 days of history are above 75 EUR, reset the baseline forecast to at least 78 EUR before applying trend adjustments."
  ]
}
